[38;2;124;124;124m5. Total: 5n + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the proof, I need to show point 1 rigorously. Let me think about the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcleanest way to do this.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFrom the computational analysis:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Sub-case 2a (b < a): No valid squares (without origin as vertex).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Sub-case 2b (b > a): Only origin-centered squares.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Sub-case 2c (b = a): All squares have origin as a vertex.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the key claims are:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- In sub-case 2a, any valid square must have the origin as a vertex.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- In sub-case 2b, any valid square is centered at the origin.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- In sub-case 2c, any valid square has the origin as a vertex.[0m

